WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.335
-Our next guest is
a best-selling author,

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:04.537
entrepreneur,
and the woman you wish

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:05.938
would host all your parties.

00:00:05.938 --> 00:00:08.975
You can now shop her lifestyle
on martha.com.

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:11.010
Please welcome back to the show
one of our favorites,

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.645
Martha Stewart, everybody.

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:14.514
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:23.756 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:32.998 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:32.998 --> 00:00:35.134
Welcome back. How are you?

00:00:35.134 --> 00:00:37.137
-I've missed you.
-I missed you, too.

00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:39.405
Do you have a nicer pool
than Chelsea Handler?

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:41.407
Yeah, I thought so.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:41.407 --> 00:00:43.143
-And my new pool is really nice.

00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:45.344
-Oh, you already have
a new pool as well.

00:00:45.344 --> 00:00:47.813
Look at you.
-[ Posh voice ] It's very fancy.

00:00:47.813 --> 00:00:50.316
-[ Laughs ]
Hey, I read something about you

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:51.951
that I found fascinating
that I wanted to ask you about.

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:54.654
You said one of
your biggest regrets was

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:56.455
that you did not host "SNL."

00:00:56.455 --> 00:00:58.824
So this was something you were
asked to do and you said no to?

00:00:58.824 --> 00:01:01.260
-Yeah. Emerging from jail...
-Yes.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.865
-"Yail, emerging from "Yail."
-Yes. You're a "Yail" graduate.

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:07.033
-Graduate.
-Yeah.

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.367
[ Laughter ]

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:11.504
-I was asked to host the show,

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:13.172
and I was so excited

00:01:13.172 --> 00:01:16.976
and my parole officer
would not let me have the time.

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:18.344
-Really?

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:20.012
-I hate that guy.
-Yeah.

00:01:20.012 --> 00:01:22.582
[ Laughter ]
-I hate him.

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:24.817
It was the right time,
it was the perfect time,

00:01:24.817 --> 00:01:26.953
I was in a good mood,
I was thin.

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:28.254
[ Laughter ]

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:29.689
-That's what
a lot of people say.

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:33.692
You do a couple weeks in "Yail,"
you're gonna lose the weight.

00:01:33.692 --> 00:01:35.128
[ Laughs ]

00:01:35.128 --> 00:01:36.729
-Anyway.
-Yes.

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:38.431
-Onward and upward.
-Onward and upward.

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:39.966
You have a new website,
martha.com.

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:42.234
-I do.
-What can we find there?

00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:46.339
-Well, all things that I
designed, things that I curate,

00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:50.276
things that I think you should
know about, content, you know,

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:51.677
overflowing content.

00:01:51.677 --> 00:01:53.413
-Is there a product
that you think

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:55.782
I in particular really need?

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:57.283
-There's lots of things,
lots of things.

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.185
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:01.855
-Knowing you for so many years,
I know that there are things

00:02:01.855 --> 00:02:04.123
that you would find interesting,
useful,

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:06.892
and you're gardening now
and, you know, you're doing

00:02:06.892 --> 00:02:09.028
all those things,
so there's all that stuff.

00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:11.764
-I have a robust gardening
career right now.

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:13.333
-Oh, good, good.
What are you growing?

00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:14.968
-I don't know what you're
talking about, yeah.

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:18.071
[ Laughter ]

00:02:18.071 --> 00:02:20.472
-Well, I thought
you were a gardener.

00:02:20.472 --> 00:02:22.341
-No!
-Oh, who does that?

00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:24.110
-Who does my gardening?
-Yeah.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:25.511
-Well, I don't know.

00:02:25.511 --> 00:02:27.079
-People? They come in?

00:02:27.079 --> 00:02:29.047
-I don't have -- what do you --
I don't have a garden.

00:02:29.047 --> 00:02:32.685
I feel like you're once again
confusing me with you.

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:35.788
-I must be.
-Yeah, you must be.

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:39.125
So you had a -- This made
a quite a bit of news.

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:41.727
You had a tag sale at your home.

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:43.128
Now, first of all, what is --

00:02:43.128 --> 00:02:45.431
-You did not come.
-I did not come.

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:48.100
-Guess guess who sent
his emissaries.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.168
-Who sent emissaries?

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:50.803
-Jimmy Fallon.
-Jimmy Fallon?

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:53.906
-He sent his decorator.
-He sent his decorator?

00:02:53.906 --> 00:02:56.976
-Yep, and she bought five
beautiful spool beds.

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:59.312
-Spool beds?
-Yeah, beautiful beds.

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:01.347
They're doing, like, a camp room
for the kids.

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:04.951
-I told you years ago, when
the spool beds come available --

00:03:04.951 --> 00:03:07.120
-I know, and you --
-Call me directly.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.121
-And you did not show up.
-I did not show up.

00:03:09.121 --> 00:03:12.825
-Actually, there were so many
people, it was a huge success,

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:14.560
and I'm never gonna
have another one.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.396
-So tell me, what is --
For those for those

00:03:17.396 --> 00:03:19.232
who are maybe on the outside
looking in,

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:22.267
what's the difference between,
say, a tag sale and a yard sale?

00:03:22.267 --> 00:03:23.436
-Not much.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:25.204
-Not much. It's fancier.
-A fancy way to say --

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:27.640
-Well, we had
a very fancy yard sale.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.676
-Okay, gotcha.
-Tag sale, garage sale,

00:03:30.676 --> 00:03:33.613
estate sale -- it was sort of a
combination of all those things.

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:35.314
-Gotcha.
How many items did you sell?

00:03:35.314 --> 00:03:36.916
-Many thousands.
-Wow!

00:03:36.916 --> 00:03:38.651
-Yeah, many thousands.
-Many thousands.

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.720
-And we had a few thousand
people come.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.122
They had to buy tickets.
It was all for philanthropy.

00:03:43.122 --> 00:03:44.790
-That's lovely.
-Yeah, so we raised

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.527
a lot of money for my for
my hospital at Mount Sinai.

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:49.795
-And when you look through your
items before you put them

00:03:49.795 --> 00:03:54.300
out for sale, what was
the strangest thing you found?

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:57.770
-Oh, well, I had so many
buildings full of stuff

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.071
that I really wanted to
get rid of,

00:03:59.071 --> 00:04:02.207
but one thing that almost got
sold and then the person

00:04:02.207 --> 00:04:04.510
who had picked it up,
gave it back to me

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:08.715
was my daughter Alexis's health
record from when she was born.

00:04:08.715 --> 00:04:13.886
Those little books that you fill
out -- roseola, measles shots,

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:15.421
all those nice, little things

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:17.223
that you really would like
to remember.

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:18.224
-Yeah.

00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:19.892
-[ Laughs ] Roseola.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:22.027
-Did you accidentally
put it for sale?

00:04:22.027 --> 00:04:24.330
-Somebody put it for sale.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.431
There were other people
going through my stuff.

00:04:26.431 --> 00:04:29.535
-Gotcha. Okay, good.
So this wasn't that somebody --

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:31.504
So the person returned it
as a favor.

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:32.705
-Yeah. They did.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:34.340
-They were gonna take it,
but they gave it back.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.775
-That's very sweet.
-But somebody took

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:38.377
my gardening cart, and I didn't
want to sell that one,

00:04:38.377 --> 00:04:40.713
and that got sold,
and I couldn't take it back.

00:04:40.713 --> 00:04:43.682
-But you will hunt them down
and find them?

00:04:43.682 --> 00:04:44.851
-Definitely.
-Yeah.

00:04:44.851 --> 00:04:46.084
[ Laughter ]

00:04:46.084 --> 00:04:48.254
-When I need the cart for
a TV show or something.

00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:49.622
-Now, this is very exciting.

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:52.592
-But it's on TV, we --
-Yeah, you filmed the tag sale.

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:54.594
So it's gonna be
a special this week.

00:04:54.594 --> 00:04:56.095
Was everybody well-behaved?
Because sometimes --

00:04:56.095 --> 00:04:57.262
-No!
-Was this like one of those

00:04:57.262 --> 00:04:59.365
Black Friday things
but in a beautiful lawn?

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:03.235
-No, no, there were some people
who -- There was a big --

00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:04.637
Well, you'll see it on the show.
-Okay.

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:05.871
-You should watch the show.

00:05:05.871 --> 00:05:07.473
-Were there limits for how much
people could buy?

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:08.941
-No.
-Okay. Yeah.

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:12.645
-But there was a limit to the
stuff and the people who came

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:15.715
on the second day, because they
hadn't gotten their money in

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:17.750
soon enough for the first day.
-Yeah.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.384
-Some of them were a little
pissed off about

00:05:19.384 --> 00:05:21.721
not having enough stuff for sale
on the second day.

00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:23.422
But, you know, that's the nature
of a tag sale.

00:05:23.422 --> 00:05:24.790
-Yeah.
-You know, you sell out.

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:26.192
You hope you sell out.
-Yeah.

00:05:26.192 --> 00:05:28.861
Get with the program, everybody.
-Right.

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:30.129
-I have to ask you about this.

00:05:30.129 --> 00:05:31.964
I'm very excited about
this news.

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:34.133
You're gonna make a maze.

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:35.667
-An amazing maze.

00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:37.636
-So this is like
a hedgerow maze?

00:05:37.636 --> 00:05:38.805
-Yes.
-Okay.

00:05:38.805 --> 00:05:42.908
-Each row is of a different type
of tree or shrub.

00:05:42.908 --> 00:05:45.377
-Okay.
-It's my idea for a maze.

00:05:45.377 --> 00:05:47.413
I've been thinking about a maze
for a long time,

00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:49.448
and I have a 3-acre paddock

00:05:49.448 --> 00:05:53.619
that I'm taking away
from my horses to do this maze.

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:55.454
-Have you broken it
to the horses yet?

00:05:55.454 --> 00:05:59.124
-Well, I took -- I planted like
four big rows so far,

00:05:59.124 --> 00:06:02.161
and I took my horse on a ride
through the maze.

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:04.663
We got lost already,
which is great.

00:06:04.663 --> 00:06:07.032
And it's like dressage.
You know, you go in there

00:06:07.032 --> 00:06:10.502
and you have to make these sharp
turns, and he actually liked it

00:06:10.502 --> 00:06:12.271
because it makes them think.
-Yeah.

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:14.974
-And so I can't wait to get
the whole thing done.

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:17.276
But the largest maze
in the world is

00:06:17.276 --> 00:06:20.012
the Dole pineapple maze
in Hawaii.

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:20.979
-I think we all know that.

00:06:20.979 --> 00:06:22.481
-Did you?
[ Laughter ]

00:06:22.481 --> 00:06:23.883
2.2 acres.

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:24.951
-2.2 acres.

00:06:24.951 --> 00:06:27.319
-And this paddock is 3 acres,
so...

00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:29.088
-You're gonna set
a Guinness world record?

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:31.090
-Yep, that's the idea.
-That's really exciting.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:33.392
-Yep, but I have to find enough
plant material.

00:06:33.392 --> 00:06:34.893
It's hard to find
that many trees.

00:06:34.893 --> 00:06:37.897
I've already planted
like 600 trees in there.

00:06:37.897 --> 00:06:39.965
-Excuse my ignorance,
but you're planting --

00:06:39.965 --> 00:06:41.300
I'm assuming the trees
you're planting

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:43.202
are already grown trees?
-Well, some of them are

00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:46.172
3 feet tall,
and they'll get denser.

00:06:46.172 --> 00:06:48.207
And the boxwoods were only about
this tall.

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:53.111
But I'm doing yews and boxwood
and proteas and beech trees

00:06:53.111 --> 00:06:56.949
and hemlocks -- not hemlocks --
and hornbeams.

00:06:56.949 --> 00:06:58.751
So I'm doing a lot of different
plant material.

00:06:58.751 --> 00:06:59.752
It's colorful, too.

00:06:59.752 --> 00:07:01.354
-That's really exciting.
-Yes.

00:07:01.354 --> 00:07:03.789
-You should do your next
tag sale in the maze.

00:07:03.789 --> 00:07:05.324
[ Laughter ]

00:07:05.324 --> 00:07:09.695
And then instead of people
buying tickets to get in,

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:11.497
you sell them tickets
to get out.

00:07:11.497 --> 00:07:14.934
-But I think what's gonna happen
is, I think,

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:18.270
once people start to think about
mazes again --

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.573
this was a medieval
kind of enjoyment.

00:07:20.573 --> 00:07:23.976
Mazes and all the castles
had mazes.

00:07:23.976 --> 00:07:26.912
If you look up history of mazes,
it's really kind of interesting,

00:07:26.912 --> 00:07:29.548
but -- and right now,
all throughout the world,

00:07:29.548 --> 00:07:33.919
there are some very interesting
mazes available for visits.

00:07:33.919 --> 00:07:38.490
But I don't know of
a really modern one.

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:40.993
So this is going to be
a fun thing.

00:07:40.993 --> 00:07:44.596
[ Laughter ]

00:07:44.596 --> 00:07:46.232
You could grow one, too.

00:07:46.232 --> 00:07:47.934
-I am not gonna make a maze.

00:07:47.934 --> 00:07:49.402
[ Laughter ]

00:07:49.402 --> 00:07:51.003
-You might.
-I might, you're right.

00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:52.204
You know what?

00:07:52.204 --> 00:07:53.705
There's a lot of things
I said I'd never do.

00:07:53.705 --> 00:07:55.508
-You might want to lose the kids
sometime.

00:07:55.508 --> 00:07:58.510
-Yeah, that's true.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:00.078
-People are gonna
get lost in it.

00:08:00.078 --> 00:08:02.214
-I'm gonna send them over to
your house and be like,

00:08:02.214 --> 00:08:05.651
"You're going over to Aunt
Martha's, go in the maze!

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:07.552
See you on Monday!"
-They are totally welcome.

00:08:07.552 --> 00:08:09.455
-Martha Stewart, everybody.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:09.455 --> 00:08:13.192
You can check out her beautiful
website now at martha.com.

00:08:13.192 --> 00:08:15.194 line:0%
We'll be right back
with Allegra Hyde.

